# The extractor answers two questions about a module and nothing else: WHAT DOES IT EXPOSE,
# and WHICH COMMENT DOCUMENTS EACH OF THOSE DECLARATIONS. It renders nothing. There is no
# indent here, no column budget, no colour, no terminal — `zerg doc`'s typography is
# cmd/doc_render.zg's, and issue #20's HTML will be a SECOND rendering of exactly what comes
# out of here. A fact about the source that only one of those two could read would be a fact
# the other one has to re-derive, which is how a project ends up with two extractors that
# disagree about what a module contains.
#
# It lives in this module rather than beside the command because of one function:
# `tokenize_trivia` (lexer.zg) is private here, and it is the only honest way to find a
# comment. The alternative — scanning the source text for `#` — reads the `#` inside a string
# literal as the start of a comment, and a documentation tool that lies about the source is
# worse than no documentation tool. The lexer already separates the two, already separates a
# `#[…]` decorator (Kind.Hash) from a comment, and already carries a line number on every
# token, so the attachment below is line arithmetic over a stream that has done the reading.
#
# WHAT A COMMENT IS ATTACHED TO is decided by geometry, in two passes over that stream:
#
#   1. the token stream is reduced to three facts per line — is there a doc RUN ending here,
#      does this line carry CODE, is this line inside a DECORATOR — and a run is a block of
#      whole-line comments on consecutive lines. A comment with code before it on its line is
#      a trailing note (`}  # so far so good`) and is dropped: it belongs to the line it sits
#      on, and stitching it onto whatever is declared below would attribute a remark about a
#      closing brace to the next function.
#
#   2. every declaration — private ones included — claims the run that ENDS ON THE LINE ABOVE
#      IT, stepping over any `#[derive(Eq)]` in between, since a decorator standing between a
#      comment and its declaration does not break the adjacency a reader sees. A blank line
#      does break it, and needs no rule of its own: a blank line emits no token, so the run
#      simply ends further up than the line above.
#
# A RUN THAT OPENS WITH A SECTION BANNER — `# --- levels ------` — DOCUMENTS NOTHING, and the
# WHOLE run goes, not just the banner line. A banner is a mark on the FILE: it divides the
# source into chapters for someone reading the source, and what a chapter is called is not what
# the first declaration under it is called. What follows the banner is the CHAPTER'S prose, and
# it is prose about the group — `log.zg`'s "the two formats, and where each is chosen" goes on
# to talk about `Format`, about `Logger.col`, and about which default zerolog chose — so
# keeping it and dropping only the dashes would hand one declaration a paragraph written about
# several. Dropped, `enum Level` reads as undocumented, which is what it is; kept, it read as
# documented by 89 characters of dashes, which is the failure this tool exists to prevent.
#
# The private declarations take part in pass 2 although nothing prints them, because the
# MODULE HEADER is defined as the run nothing claimed: the first run in the file, with no code
# above it, still unclaimed once every declaration has taken its own. That is what tells
# `strings.zg`'s file header — which sits flush against `import "ascii"`, with no blank line
# anywhere near it — from a comment that documents the first declaration. An import claims
# nothing: it binds a namespace, it declares no member of this module's surface, and a reader
# looking at the top of a file is reading about the file.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT:
#
#   - a second type printer. Every type is spelled by `ty_name` (ast.zg) and every
#     spec-with-arguments by `spec_key`, because a signature in a document that disagreed with
#     a signature in a diagnostic would be one of them lying about the language.
#   - a second parser. `doc_lit` prints a default value only when it is a LITERAL, reading the
#     surface text the literal node already carries, and prints `…` for everything else. A
#     document's signature answers "how do I call this"; it does not promise to evaluate.
#   - an evaluator of examples. A ```zerg``` fence is carried through as written. Running one
#     is `--check`'s job, and re-writing one for a reader is issue #18's.
#   - silent about what it could not read. A file whose parse RAISES still yields its header —
#     the header rule needs no AST — and the failure is recorded in `DocModule.broken` for the
#     renderer to print. `src/stdlib/atomic.zg` is the live case: E9004 comes from the PARSER
#     (a generic struct), so a doc tool that let the raise through would drop a whole module,
#     and one that swallowed it would drop the module's declarations without saying so.

# --- what a document is -------------------------------------------------------------------

# DocModule is one module's whole document. It is extracted once and rendered many ways, so it
# carries what the SOURCE says and nothing about how it is shown.
pub struct DocModule {
	# the name the reader asked for, which is the document's title
	pub name: str

	# the module header comment, stripped of its `#` markers; empty when the file opens with
	# a declaration or with nothing at all
	pub header: list[str]

	# every exposed declaration, in SOURCE ORDER — the order the author put them in, which
	# this tree treats as part of the document (`strings.zg` keeps the trim family together,
	# and its header narrates the file in the order the file is written). Finding one
	# declaration by name is `doc_find`'s job, and alphabetical order is that question's,
	# not this list's.
	pub decls: list[DocDecl]

	# one honest sentence per file whose parse raised. Empty is the normal case; when it is
	# not, a renderer that dropped it would turn a missing chapter into a silence.
	pub broken: list[str]
}

# DocDecl is one exposed declaration: what it is, what it is called, how it is called, and
# what was written above it.
pub struct DocDecl {
	# "fn" | "struct" | "enum" | "type" | "const" | "spec" — WHAT is declared, which is the
	# section a renderer files it under. It is a `str` and not an enum because the readers do
	# three things with it: group by it, print it, and compare it. An enum crossing the module
	# boundary would have to publish every variant to buy a spelling check on six words that
	# are already a string beside them.
	#
	# It is the word `sig` leads with wherever the language HAS a keyword for the form, but it
	# is not promised to be: a signature may carry a modifier in front of it (`unsafe fn`), and
	# a module-level binding is `const`, `mut`, or no keyword at all while all three are filed
	# under "const". A renderer that emphasises the name checks before it counts.
	pub kind: str

	# the bare declared name — `split`, `Logger`, `level`
	pub name: str

	# a method's receiver type, "" for everything else. The parser flattens `impl T { … }`
	# into ordinary functions carrying a receiver (ast.zg, FnDecl), and this follows the AST
	# rather than re-nesting them: a method is a top-level DocDecl with `recv` set, which the
	# renderer groups under its type and `doc_find` reaches as `Logger.level` in one scan.
	pub recv: str

	# the signature as plain text, `pub` omitted. Everything in a document is exposed by
	# definition, so the marker is noise on every line; dropping it also leaves all six forms
	# beginning with their keyword, which is what lets a renderer find the name to emphasise
	# by counting characters instead of parsing the string back.
	pub sig: str

	# the attached comment, stripped; empty means UNDOCUMENTED, and a renderer must say so
	# rather than leave a gap the reader cannot tell from a rendering bug
	pub doc: list[str]

	# the struct's public fields, the enum's variants, or the spec's required signatures —
	# each a line and its own comment. It is deliberately one level deep: none of the three
	# has anything below it, and a recursive container would invite a shape no source has.
	pub members: list[DocMember]

	# how many fields were NOT listed because they are private. Go's `contains unexported
	# fields` note, for the same reason: they are not interface, but a document that showed a
	# struct with two of its six fields would be describing a constructor call that does not
	# compile. The count is taken here, where every field is visible; the wording is the
	# renderer's.
	pub hidden: int

	# where it was declared. The `--check` that runs a document's examples has to report a
	# failure at a place, and the place is in hand exactly here — afterwards nobody can
	# recover it. This is ConstDecl's argument for carrying its own position, one layer up.
	pub path: str
	pub line: int
}

# DocMember is one field, variant, or required signature: a line, and whatever was written
# above it.
pub struct DocMember {
	pub sig: str
	pub doc: list[str]
}

# --- extraction -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# doc_module extracts one module's document from its sources. `srcs` and `paths` are row for
# row — the pairing `load_sources_at` already keeps — because attaching a comment is per-FILE
# work and a file that will not parse has to be named in the sentence that says so.
#
# EVERY FILE IS PARSED UNDER `guard`. A module that cannot be parsed must still yield its
# header and an honest marker, never a silent absence; `atomic` is that module today. The
# diagnostic's own text cannot come back through a `guard`, so the sentence is a general one
# pointing at where the module's status is already written down.
pub fn doc_module(name: str, srcs: list[str], paths: list[str]) -> DocModule {
	# the two lists are ONE table, and a caller that hands over a short one would otherwise
	# leave an `IndexError` to come out of a `pub` function with nothing said about whose
	# mistake it was
	raise p_impossible(f"doc_module was given {srcs.len()} sources and {paths.len()} paths — they are one table, a source and the file it was read from, and a document cannot name a file it was not told about") if srcs.len() != paths.len()

	mut header: list[str] = []
	mut decls: list[DocDecl] = []
	mut broken: list[str] = []

	mut i := 0
	for i < srcs.len() {
		path := paths[i]
		mut tv := doc_trivia(srcs[i])
		parsed := guard {
			parse_at(srcs[i], path)
		}
		if f := parsed {
			doc_file_decls(f, tv, decls)
		} else {
			broken.append(f"{path} does not parse under this compiler; its declarations are not listed (see docs/runtime/stdlib.md)")
		}

		# after the claim pass, never before it: a header is the run no declaration wanted
		hd := doc_header(tv)
		if hd.len() > 0 {
			if header.len() > 0 {
				header.append("")
			}
			for line in hd {
				header.append(line)
			}
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return DocModule(name, header, decls, broken)
}

# doc_find answers WHERE in `m.decls` the named declaration is. `name` is `split` or
# `Logger.level` — a method is reached by the same key the renderer groups it under, and the
# comparison is case sensitive because an identifier is.
#
# It answers EVERY declaration with that key, not the first. One key can name two declarations:
# a module may hold `pub struct Pair` and `pub fn Pair`, which `zerg lint` accepts and which
# the language keeps apart by position, and a directory module is several files whose names
# only have to be unique to the linker. Answering the first one in source order made the second
# unreachable AND unmentioned — `zerg doc mod.Pair` printed the struct and said nothing about
# the function — which is the silent omission this whole tool exists to prevent. What to do
# with two answers is the command's; what there are is this.
#
# It answers INDICES rather than `DocDecl` rows so that the caller can render a hit together
# with what belongs to it: a type's entry is the type plus the methods that follow it in the
# same list, which needs the position and not just the row.
pub fn doc_find(m: DocModule, name: str) -> list[int] {
	mut out: list[int] = []
	mut i := 0
	for i < m.decls.len() {
		d := m.decls[i]
		mut key := d.name
		if d.recv != "" {
			key = f"{d.recv}.{d.name}"
		}
		if key == name {
			out.append(i)
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return out
}

# doc_first_sentence is the one-line summary that a brief listing and a module index both
# show: the first paragraph flattened onto one line, cut at the first `.` that ends a word.
#
# It lives beside the extraction rather than in either renderer because both of them ask it,
# and issue #20's HTML will be the third — two copies of "what is a sentence" would drift into
# two different summaries of the same comment. A fence ends the paragraph: an example is not
# a description, and the first line of one reads as a fragment.
pub fn doc_first_sentence(doc: list[str]) -> str {
	mut para := ""
	for line in doc {
		break if line == ""
		break if doc_is_fence(line)

		if para != "" {
			para = para + " "
		}
		para = para + line
	}

	b := bytearray(para)
	mut i := 0
	for i < b.len() {
		if b[i] == b'.' {
			return fstr_slice(b, 0, i + 1) if i + 1 == b.len() or b[i + 1] == b' '
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return para
}

fn doc_is_fence(line: str) -> bool {
	b := bytearray(line)
	return false if b.len() < 3
	return b[0] == b'`' and b[1] == b'`' and b[2] == b'`'
}

# doc_is_banner reports whether a comment line is a section banner — `--- levels ---------`,
# the form all two hundred of them in this tree are written in. It is asked of a run's FIRST
# line only, and the file header explains why the whole run goes with it.
#
# The shape is required at both ends: `--- ` opens it, and it closes on the dashes that pad it
# out to the source's right margin. A paragraph that happens to begin with an em-rule does not
# end in one, and a line of prose does not begin with three hyphens and a space.
fn doc_is_banner(line: str) -> bool {
	b := bytearray(line)

	return false if b.len() < 8
	return false if b[0] != b'-' or b[1] != b'-' or b[2] != b'-' or b[3] != b' '

	return b[b.len() - 1] == b'-' and b[b.len() - 2] == b'-' and b[b.len() - 3] == b'-'
}

# --- the declarations of one file -----------------------------------------------------------

# doc_file_decls walks one parsed file, claims a comment for EVERY declaration in it, and
# appends the exposed ones to `out` in source order.
#
# The private ones are walked for their claim alone. That is what keeps `doc_header` honest —
# a run a private function owns is not the file's header — and it costs one pass either way.
# An `impl` head claims too: it declares nothing this document lists, but a comment above
# `impl Display for Meters` is about that impl, and if the impl were the first thing in a file
# the comment would otherwise be read as the module's header.
#
# The file's own lists are per KIND, so nothing in them is where the author left it; they are
# collected and then put back into source order by `doc_sorted`.
fn doc_file_decls(f: File, mut &tv: DocTrivia, mut &out: list[DocDecl]) {
	mut ds: list[DocDecl] = []

	for c in f.consts {
		doc := doc_attach(tv, c.line)
		mut none: list[DocMember] = []
		if c.is_pub {
			ds.append(DocDecl("const", c.name, "", doc_const_sig(c), doc, none, 0, c.path, c.line))
		}
	}

	for s in f.structs {
		doc := doc_attach(tv, s.line)
		mut ms: list[DocMember] = []
		mut hidden := 0
		for fd in s.fields {
			fdoc := doc_attach(tv, fd.line)
			if fd.is_pub {
				ms.append(DocMember(doc_field_sig(fd), fdoc))
			} else {
				hidden = hidden + 1
			}
		}
		if s.is_pub {
			ds.append(DocDecl("struct", s.name, "", "struct " + s.name, doc, ms, hidden, s.path, s.line))
		}
	}

	for e in f.enums {
		doc := doc_attach(tv, e.line)
		mut ms: list[DocMember] = []
		for vd in e.variants {
			vdoc := doc_attach(tv, vd.line)
			ms.append(DocMember(doc_variant_sig(vd), vdoc))
		}
		if e.is_pub {
			ds.append(DocDecl("enum", e.name, "", "enum " + e.name, doc, ms, 0, e.path, e.line))
		}
	}

	for t in f.types {
		doc := doc_attach(tv, t.line)
		mut none: list[DocMember] = []
		if t.is_pub {
			ds.append(DocDecl("type", t.name, "", f"type {t.name} = {ty_name(t.under)}", doc, none, 0, t.path, t.line))
		}
	}

	for sp in f.specs {
		doc := doc_attach(tv, sp.line)
		mut ms: list[DocMember] = []
		for req in sp.reqs {
			rdoc := doc_attach(tv, req.line)
			ms.append(DocMember(doc_fn_sig(req), rdoc))
		}
		if sp.is_pub {
			ds.append(DocDecl("spec", sp.name, "", doc_spec_sig(sp), doc, ms, 0, sp.path, sp.line))
		}
	}

	for ip in f.impls {
		doc_claim(tv, ip.line)
	}

	for fd in f.fns {
		doc := doc_attach(tv, fd.line)
		mut none: list[DocMember] = []
		if fd.is_pub {
			ds.append(DocDecl("fn", fd.name, fd.recv, doc_fn_sig(fd), doc, none, 0, fd.path, fd.line))
		}
	}

	for d in doc_sorted(ds) {
		out.append(d)
	}
}

# doc_sorted puts one file's declarations back into source order.
#
# Insertion sort by adjacent swaps, for the reason `sym_sorted` (ast.zg) gives about the same
# job on the same data: a file's top level is tens of declarations, and what matters is that
# the sort is STABLE, so two declarations reported at one line keep the order they were
# collected in instead of trading places between runs.
fn doc_sorted(ds: list[DocDecl]) -> list[DocDecl] {
	mut out: list[DocDecl] = []
	for d in ds {
		out.append(d)
		mut j := out.len() - 1
		for j > 0 {
			break if out[j].line >= out[j - 1].line
			tmp := out[j - 1]
			out[j - 1] = out[j]
			out[j] = tmp
			j = j - 1
		}
	}
	return out
}

# --- signatures -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# doc_fn_sig spells a function the way its declaration is written, without the `pub`.
#
# A METHOD DROPS ITS FIRST PARAMETER: the parser prepends `this: T` when it flattens an
# `impl`, so printing the list as the AST holds it would show a receiver nobody writes at the
# call site. The receiver survives as `DocDecl.recv`, which is where the renderer reads it.
#
# A FUNCTION DECLARED IN A MODULE-LEVEL `unsafe { … }` GROUP IS AN `unsafe fn`, and says so.
# GRAMMAR group 12 gives the group that meaning — "unsafe throughout its body, callable only
# from unsafe" — and group 5 gives the single-function form the same spelling, so this is the
# language's own word for it and not a marker invented here. Leaving it off would document a
# function as callable from anywhere when it is callable from one place.
fn doc_fn_sig(fd: FnDecl) -> str {
	mut out := doc_fn_word(fd) + fd.name
	if fd.tparams.len() > 0 {
		out = out + "["
		mut i := 0
		for i < fd.tparams.len() {
			if i > 0 {
				out = out + ", "
			}
			out = out + fd.tparams[i]
			if fd.tbounds[i] != "" {
				out = out + ": " + doc_bound(fd.tbounds[i])
			}
			i = i + 1
		}
		out = out + "]"
	}

	out = out + "("
	mut k := 0
	if fd.recv != "" {
		k = 1
	}
	mut first := true
	for k < fd.params.len() {
		p := fd.params[k]
		if not first {
			out = out + ", "
		}
		first = false
		if p.is_ref {
			out = out + "mut &"
		}
		out = out + p.name + ": " + ty_name(p.ty)
		if p.has_def {
			out = out + " = " + doc_lit(p.def)
		}
		k = k + 1
	}
	out = out + ")"

	return f"{out} -> {ty_name(fd.ret)}" if fd.has_ret
	return out
}

# doc_bound reads a type parameter's bound back into the source's spelling. A bound is a
# CONJUNCTION carried joined by `+` in one string (ast.zg, FnDecl.tbounds), and `c_split_bounds`
# is the one function that takes it apart — the emitter's, because that is where a bound is
# checked, and a second splitter would be a second opinion about where a spec name ends.
fn doc_fn_word(fd: FnDecl) -> str {
	return "unsafe fn " if fd.in_unsafe
	return "fn "
}

fn doc_bound(bound: str) -> str {
	mut out := ""
	for sp in c_split_bounds(bound) {
		if out != "" {
			out = out + " + "
		}
		out = out + sp
	}
	return out
}

# doc_const_sig spells a module-level binding with the keyword that was WRITTEN, and with none
# when none was.
#
# It used to print `const` for all three forms, on the argument that a module-level binding is
# the module's immutable state (docs/runtime/package.md). A `mut` binding inside a module-level
# `unsafe { … }` group is precisely what that argument is the exception to (GRAMMAR group 12),
# so the one mutable global the language has was documented as the one thing it is not.
#
# `in_unsafe` is deliberately NOT spelled here, and it is the only fact about the declaration
# this signature leaves out. GRAMMAR group 12 says in as many words that there is no `unsafe
# mut` prefix, so writing one would put into a document a form the language will not read; and
# what the group grants a binding is exactly the `mut`, which is already the first word. A
# function is the other way round — `unsafe fn` IS a spelling (group 5) — which is why
# `doc_fn_sig` prints it and this does not.
fn doc_const_sig(c: ConstDecl) -> str {
	head := doc_bind_word(c) + c.name
	return f"{head}: {ty_name(c.ty)} = {doc_lit(c.value)}" if c.has_ty
	return f"{head} := {doc_lit(c.value)}"
}

fn doc_bind_word(c: ConstDecl) -> str {
	return "const " if c.is_const
	return "mut " if c.is_mut

	return ""
}

fn doc_field_sig(fd: FieldDef) -> str {
	out := f"{fd.name}: {ty_name(fd.ty)}"
	return f"{out} = {doc_lit(fd.def)}" if fd.has_def
	return out
}

fn doc_variant_sig(vd: VariantDef) -> str {
	mut out := vd.name
	if vd.fields.len() > 0 {
		out = out + "(" + ty_names(vd.fields) + ")"
	}
	return f"{out} = {doc_lit(vd.discr)}" if vd.has_discr
	return out
}

fn doc_spec_sig(sp: SpecDecl) -> str {
	mut out := "spec " + sp.name
	if sp.params.len() > 0 {
		out = out + "[" + join_commas(sp.params) + "]"
	}
	mut i := 0
	for i < sp.supers.len() {
		if i == 0 {
			out = out + ": "
		} else {
			out = out + " + "
		}
		out = out + spec_key(sp.supers[i], sp.super_args[i])
		i = i + 1
	}
	return out
}

# doc_lit prints a default value when it IS a literal and `…` when it is not.
#
# It reads the surface text the literal node already kept (the invariant ast.zg opens with) and
# it does not recurse: a call, a name, a negation are all `…`. That is not a gap to be filled
# later — a signature in a document says how to CALL something, and a default built out of an
# expression is a fact about the source, which the `…` sends the reader to.
fn doc_lit(e: Expr) -> str {
	return match e {
		Expr.EInt(lex, _, _) => lex
		Expr.EFloat(lex) => lex
		Expr.EStr(v) => f"\"{v}\""
		Expr.EBool(v) => doc_bool(v)
		Expr.ENil => "nil"
		_ => "…"
	}
}

fn doc_bool(v: bool) -> str {
	return "true" if v
	return "false"
}

# --- comments, on the token stream --------------------------------------------------------

# DocRun is one block of whole-line comments on consecutive lines — a candidate for being some
# declaration's documentation, or the file's header.
struct DocRun {
	pub first: int
	pub last: int
	pub lines: list[str]
}

# DocTrivia is the whole first pass: the runs a file holds, and two facts per LINE that decide
# which declaration each run belongs to. `taken` runs beside `runs` and records the claim, so
# a run answers exactly one declaration and `doc_header` can ask what is left.
#
# `code`, `deco` and `imp` are indexed BY LINE NUMBER, one slot per line of the file plus a
# spare, so the arithmetic below can read the line above or below without a bound test at every
# step.
struct DocTrivia {
	pub runs: list[DocRun]
	pub taken: list[bool]
	pub code: list[bool]
	pub deco: list[bool]

	# whether the line holds an `import`. It is asked lexically, off Kind.Import, rather than
	# from `File.imports`, so that a file whose parse RAISED can still be asked — which is the
	# only condition under which a module has a header and no declarations at all.
	pub imp: list[bool]
}

# doc_trivia is the first pass: one walk of the lexed-with-comments stream.
#
# A `#[…]` decorator is Kind.Hash and not a comment — the lexer separated them long before
# this — and its lines are marked as BOTH code and decorator: code, because something is
# written there, and decorator, because the claim in pass two steps over exactly those lines.
# The bracket depth is what carries a decorator that spans lines.
#
# The ASI semicolons and the Eof are neither: they are punctuation the lexer inserted, and a
# line that holds nothing else is a blank line to a reader.
fn doc_trivia(src: str) -> DocTrivia {
	toks := tokenize_trivia(src)

	mut last_line := 1
	for t in toks {
		if t.line > last_line {
			last_line = t.line
		}
	}

	mut code: list[bool] = []
	mut deco: list[bool] = []
	mut imp: list[bool] = []
	mut n := 0
	for n <= last_line + 1 {
		code.append(false)
		deco.append(false)
		imp.append(false)
		n = n + 1
	}

	mut runs: list[DocRun] = []
	mut taken: list[bool] = []
	mut prev := 0
	mut depth := 0

	for t in toks {
		if depth > 0 {
			code[t.line] = true
			deco[t.line] = true
			prev = t.line
			if t.kind == Kind.LBrack {
				depth = depth + 1
			} else if t.kind == Kind.RBrack {
				depth = depth - 1
			}
			continue
		}

		if t.kind == Kind.Comment {
			# a note at the end of a line of code, which documents nothing below it
			continue if prev == t.line

			doc_add_line(runs, taken, t.line, doc_strip(t.lexeme))
			continue
		}

		continue if t.kind == Kind.Semi or t.kind == Kind.Eof

		code[t.line] = true
		prev = t.line
		if t.kind == Kind.Hash {
			deco[t.line] = true
			depth = 1
		} else if t.kind == Kind.Import {
			imp[t.line] = true
		}
	}
	return DocTrivia(runs, taken, code, deco, imp)
}

# doc_add_line puts one comment line into the run it continues, or opens a new run. A run is
# extended only when the line ABOVE was the previous comment line, which is what makes a blank
# line a separator without anything here looking at whitespace.
fn doc_add_line(mut &runs: list[DocRun], mut &taken: list[bool], line: int, text: str) {
	if runs.len() > 0 {
		k := runs.len() - 1
		if runs[k].last == line - 1 {
			mut r := runs[k]
			r.last = line
			r.lines.append(text)
			runs[k] = r
			return
		}
	}

	mut ls: list[str] = []
	ls.append(text)
	runs.append(DocRun(line, line, ls))
	taken.append(false)
}

# doc_strip takes the `#` off a comment, and one space after it, and any trailing whitespace.
# What is left is the text as written — the fence lines, the indentation of an example, and
# the blank line between two paragraphs all survive, because they are what the author put
# there and the renderer needs them to tell prose from a code block.
fn doc_strip(lexeme: str) -> str {
	b := bytearray(lexeme)
	mut lo := 0
	if lo < b.len() and b[lo] == b'#' {
		lo = lo + 1
	}
	if lo < b.len() and b[lo] == b' ' {
		lo = lo + 1
	}
	mut hi := b.len()
	for hi > lo and (b[hi - 1] == b' ' or b[hi - 1] == b'\t') {
		hi = hi - 1
	}
	return fstr_slice(b, lo, hi)
}

# doc_attach is pass two for one declaration: the run that ends on the line above it, stepping
# over the decorator lines in between, or nothing when the line above holds code or a blank.
#
# One run can answer only one declaration, and it cannot answer two by accident: a run's last
# line is unique, so at most one declaration is on the line below it. `taken` records the claim
# for `doc_header`, which is the only thing that asks about the runs nobody wanted.
fn doc_attach(mut &tv: DocTrivia, line: int) -> list[str] {
	mut none: list[str] = []
	at := doc_claim(tv, line)
	return none if at < 0

	# claimed and then thrown away: a section banner is taken OUT of circulation, so that
	# `doc_header` cannot pick it up either, and answers nothing
	return none if doc_is_banner(tv.runs[at].lines[0])

	return tv.runs[at].lines
}

# doc_claim is doc_attach without the text — the index of the run claimed, or -1. It is what an
# `impl` head calls: an impl takes its comment out of circulation without putting it in the
# document.
fn doc_claim(mut &tv: DocTrivia, line: int) -> int {
	mut a := line
	for a > 1 and tv.deco[a - 1] {
		a = a - 1
	}
	return -1 if a < 2

	mut i := 0
	for i < tv.runs.len() {
		if tv.runs[i].last == a - 1 and not tv.taken[i] {
			tv.taken[i] = true
			return i
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return -1
}

# doc_header is the module's own comment: the FIRST run in the file, with no code above it,
# that no declaration claimed, that is not a section banner, and with nothing but a blank line
# or an `import` under it.
#
# Each of the four conditions is doing work. FIRST, because a comment further down a file is
# about what is under it. NO CODE ABOVE, because a comment written below the first declaration
# is a remark inside the file and not a description of it. UNCLAIMED, because a file may open
# with a comment that documents its first declaration — `has_prefix`, example and all, is the
# first thing in `strings.zg` after the header — and that comment belongs to the function,
# leaving the module with no header, which is the honest answer rather than a stolen one.
#
# THE LINE BELOW is the fourth, and it is there because pass two cannot see everything that can
# be written. An INHERENT `impl T { … }` head is not in the parsed file at all: the parser
# flattens the block into methods and records an ImplDecl only for `impl Spec for T`, so a
# comment above `impl Thing {` is claimed by nobody, and the first three conditions alone would
# read it as the module's header — a remark about one type presented as the document's opening
# paragraph. Nothing may be under a header but a blank line or an import.
#
# The import half of that is not a concession but the case that fixed the whole rule:
# `strings.zg`'s header runs straight into `import "ascii"` with no blank line between them, so
# a rule phrased as "separated by a blank line" gives the most-read module in the standard
# library no header at all. An import declares no member of this module's surface and claims no
# comment; a reader looking above it is reading about the file.
fn doc_header(tv: DocTrivia) -> list[str] {
	mut none: list[str] = []

	return none if tv.runs.len() == 0
	return none if tv.taken[0]

	r := tv.runs[0]
	return none if doc_is_banner(r.lines[0])

	mut i := 1
	for i < r.first {
		return none if tv.code[i]
		i = i + 1
	}

	below := r.last + 1
	return none if tv.code[below] and not tv.imp[below]
	return r.lines
}
